<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ymer Prizreni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ymer Prizreni was an Albanian Ottoman official and leader of the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>, formed in 1878 to resist the transfer of Albanian-inhabited lands to Montenegro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He headed the league's provisional government in 1881, before the Ottoman army suppressed the movement, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ymer-prizreni.jpg" alt="Ymer Prizreni" width="225" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Albania · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ymer_Prizreni_2008_stamp_of_Albania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ymer Prizreni, also called Ymer Drini, came from a family of müderris, teachers of Islamic learning, who held land around Zgatar near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. His father, Sylejman Ali Nuhi, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of the Bajrakli Mosque in Prizren and owned land, stores and mills; his grandfather Ali Nuhi founded the first mosque in Zgatar on 10 May 1814. Ymer studied Islamic theology and law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and then taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of Prizren, where he became known as a supporter of the lower classes against the injustices of the Ottoman administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878 began, he worked with other Albanian leaders to protect the territorial integrity of Albania and to press its national rights abroad, organising a self-defence committee for the Sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and other committees in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. In 1877 he was elected to the first Ottoman Parliament, and he helped found the Central Committee for Defending Albanian Rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdyl-frasheri">Abdyl Frashëri</a>, with whom he remained connected for the rest of his life.</p><p>In the spring of 1878 he chaired the committee that organised the general assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> which founded the League of Prizren. He was elected chairman of the League in October 1879, and in January 1881 he became head of its Autonomous Provisional Government. In that office he directed the League's political work for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a> of Albania and organised its armed resistance to the Ottoman armies sent to crush it in the spring of 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the League was suppressed he escaped capture by the Ottoman authorities and, refusing to submit, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>, where he died on 12 June 1887 and where his tomb lies. He is said to have been murdered, and his archive of documents was reportedly stolen. The family's only descendants come from his brother Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ymer Prizreni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 10 June 1878 Albanian notables met in Prizren and formed a league to resist the partition, at first with tacit Ottoman approval, under leaders such as <b>Ymer Prizreni</b>, Abdyl Frashëri and Sulejman Vokshi.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-prizren">League of Prizren</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zgatar, in what is now <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.13,20.71&amp;year=1863" aria-label="Zgatar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5053 664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ymer Prizreni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5503 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5503 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.13,20.71&amp;year=1863">What was here in 1863? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
